 ## Background
-The concept of using SDFs for neural rendering emerged as a solution to the "foggy" and geometrically ambiguous outputs of standard density-based NeRFs. Azinovic et al. introduced the truncated SDF approach in April 2021, replacing the density MLP with a truncated SDF MLP to enable clean mesh reconstruction via Marching Cubes [S1]. Concurrently, Park et al. introduced Nerfies in November 2020, which used deformation fields for dynamic scenes, laying groundwork for non-rigid SDF applications [S1]. In June 2022, SparseNeuS improved upon NeuS by focusing on sparse-view SDF reconstruction using a hybrid representation of geometry encoding volumes and learnable image features [S1]. These developments shifted the focus from implicit volume density to explicit surface boundaries, allowing for better geometric fidelity and integration with traditional computer graphics pipelines like PBR [S1, 15009-sdf-based-neural-volume-rendering-with-pbr.md].
+The concept of using SDFs for neural rendering emerged as a solution to the "foggy" and geometrically ambiguous outputs of standard density-based NeRFs [S1]. Azinovic et al. introduced the truncated SDF approach in April 2021, replacing the density MLP with a truncated SDF MLP to enable clean mesh reconstruction via Marching Cubes [S1]. Concurrently, Park et al. introduced Nerfies in November 2020, which used deformation fields for dynamic scenes, laying groundwork for non-rigid SDF applications [S1]. In June 2022, SparseNeuS improved upon NeuS by focusing on sparse-view SDF reconstruction using a hybrid representation of geometry encoding volumes and learnable image features [S1]. These developments shifted the focus from implicit volume density to explicit surface boundaries, allowing for better geometric fidelity and clean reconstruction results via Marching Cubes [S1].
 
